{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791704714,"nonce":"mCth5HuDQcUDQb1feQGSp/SR9cc=","title":"Shrink-to-fit text at a fractional font size wraps at its own measured width","message":"An absolutely positioned div holding \"overflow: auto\" in monospace at 14.4px lays out 129 wide and 37 tall: two lines, broken at the space, though the width is its own max-content. At 13px and at 16px (system-ui) it is one line. Reproducer: tests/native-user-agent.mjs's last block, before its user-agent fix; or a div with `position: absolute; padding: 1px 4px; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14.4px` and the text \"a b\" (34 by 37).\n\nThe width taffy is told (the measured max-content) and the width the text is broken at disagree slightly: the measure runs in upstream Blinc's blinc_layout (tree.rs compute_layout_with_measure, text_measure_function), which blinc_abi takes as a git dependency, so the fix is there: a width a text measured, given back as the limit, must not break its line (compare with a tolerance, or round the measured width up).\n\nSeen in the wiki: an inline `code` with a space in a paragraph was two lines tall (an inline box in a flow is absolutely positioned and shrink-to-fit). Mitigated in blinc_ts's user-agent sheet: code, kbd and mark directly in a paragraph container are white-space: nowrap, which is right for a box kept whole on a line. Any other shrink-to-fit text at a fractional size can still wrap.","files":null}]}